            <ArticleTitle>Lumbar plexus nerve entrapment syndromes as a cause of groin pain in athletes.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>115-20</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1249/JSR.0b013e318214a045</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>In athletes, groin pain is not uncommon and can be severe and activity-limiting. Nerve entrapment syndromes of the lumbar plexus are a rare but important etiology that should be considered when evaluating athletes. Diagnosis can be made based on patterns of pain and hypoesthesia following the sensory distribution of the involved nerve and by pain relief with nerve block. Conservative therapies, including nerve blocks, neurodestructive procedures, and medications, may provide long-term pain relief. If nonsurgical therapies fail, referral should be made for surgical exploration and neurectomy.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
